{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_019sfCkNt2r7e7PZyCsZPEsR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context and determine what to build next.\n\n## Project Summary\n\n**Problem**: go-unix-utils is an experiment in specification-driven development using classic Unix utilities as the test bed. They derive formal specifications from C source code, generate Go implementations from those specs using an AI coding agent, and verify parity against reference binaries through differential testing.\n\n**Architecture**: Four-stage pipeline: extract → specify → synthesize → verify. Key components:\n- `cmd/` - Go implementations of each utility\n- `pkg/testutils/` - Differential testing harness\n- `pkg/sys/` - Syscall abstractions (Darwin/Linux)\n\n\n- `pkg/format/` - Output formatting\n- `magefiles/` - Build targets\n\n**Current State**: \n- All PRDs are written (prd001-prd010)\n- Use cases are written for all releases\n- Test suites are written for all releases\n- No code has been implemented yet\n- Current focus is on the specification phase\n\n**Roadmap**:\n- rel01.0 (in_progress): ts - use case rel01.0-uc001-ts is in_progress\n- rel01.1 (pending): wc, cat, sponge\n- rel02.0 (pending): ls core, du\n- rel02.1 (pending): ls extended\n\n## Priorities\n\nI need to focus on rel01.0 since it's the earliest incomplete release with just one use case: rel01.0-uc001-ts for the ts utility. Looking at the implementation order, pkg/testutils is the foundational differential testing harness that all utilities depend on, so it needs to come before cmd/ts. Since ts only requires pkg/testutils with no other dependencies, I should propose implementing pkg/testutils first following the dependency ordering principle of building libraries before consumers.\n\nThe core deliverables for pkg/testutils are the DiffTest struct, the RunDiffTests function for binary execution and comparison, normalization hooks including a TimestampNormalizer, and file-state comparison logic. This should be around 250-350 lines of production code, which fits the constraint. Following the file naming convention, the primary type is DiffTest so the file should be named difftest.go rather than testutils.go. Looking at the requirements mapping, I need to consolidate the PRD sub-requirements into task-level ones that fit the 5-8 requirement target. R1 covers the DiffTest struct with all its fields including args, stdin, env, exit code expectations, normalization hooks, and file-state comparison. R2 handles the binary execution piece. I should continue grouping the remaining requirements around comparison logic, reporting, and the normalization system to keep things balanced. that runs Go and reference binaries with identical inputs, captures stdout/stderr/exit code, supports configurable timeout and working directory, implements comparison logic with normalization and byte-for-byte checking, provides a composable normalization hook mechanism including a TimestampNormalizer, and supports file-state comparison for utilities that produce file output rather than stdout.\n\nNow I'm laying out the acceptance criteria: a cmd/ package should be able to define a test slice and call RunDiffTests with minimal setup, passing tests produce no output while failures show the triggering args and both binaries' outputs, TimestampNormalizer correctly replaces strftime patterns, file expectations fail when the Go binary writes different content, timeouts produce clear error messages, and all subtests run with proper naming.\n\nFor design decisions, I'm placing the code in pkg/testutils/ with difftest.go as the primary file, using os/exec.CommandContext with context.WithTimeout for the 10-second default timeout, implementing normalizers as a composable slice of byte-transformation functions, and handling file-state comparison through an additional field in the DiffTest struct.\n\nNow I'm mapping out the file structure: difftest.go for the core DiffTest type and test execution logic, normalize.go for the TimestampNormalizer and composition utilities, and difftest_test.go for tests—three files total, which fits within the 5-7 file constraint. The primary exported type is DiffTest, so difftest.go is correctly named per the architecture guidelines, and splitting normalization into its own file makes sense organizationally even though it's secondary.\n\nI need to propose exactly one task here. Since there's no existing implementation in pkg/testutils/, the stitch agent will need to read the PRD, ARCHITECTURE.yaml for context on design decisions and project structure, and go.mod to understand the module path. Let me draft the task with a title that includes the requirement IDs as specified. I'm working through the constraints here—I can only propose one task, but the acceptance criteria require tests, so I need to include both implementation and tests together even though the guidelines prefer splitting them. The production code should stay within the 250-350 line budget, and I'm making sure the required_reading section is comprehensive enough that the agent can implement this without needing to ask for additional context. Looking at the requirements, I need to define the DiffTest struct with fields for test arguments, stdin input, environment variables, expected exit code, normalization functions, test naming, and file state comparisons. Then I'm implementing RunDiffTests to execute both binaries with matching parameters, run each test as a subtest, and compare their outputs against expected results.\n\nFor execution, I'm using os/exec.CommandContext with a configurable timeout defaulting to 10 seconds, capturing stdout and stderr separately from each binary, and supporting an optional WorkDir that creates a temporary directory per test when not specified. The comparison logic applies the normalization functions in sequence to both outputs before doing byte-for-byte comparisons of stdout and stderr, plus exact exit code matching. When tests diverge, I'm reporting the arguments, truncated stdin, both binaries' outputs, and exit codes.\n\nNow I'm implementing a timestamp normalizer that replaces common time patterns like 'Feb 19 12:34:56' with a placeholder, and a composer function that chains multiple normalizers together. For file-state comparison, after both binaries finish, I'll read each expected file and compare its content byte-for-byte, reporting divergences in the same format as output mismatches.\n\nThe main source file is difftest.go in the testutils package. I'm using context with a 10-second timeout for binary execution and extracting exit codes from the error. The Normalize field is a slice of functions applied in sequence, so when it's empty, no normalization happens—this avoids locking into a single-function design. The timestamp normalizer uses regex to match strftime patterns like '%b %d %H:%M:%S'. I'm verifying the spec meets all the constraints—six requirements, four design decisions, six acceptance criteria across three files, all properly anchored to the PRD and self-contained without any invented details.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":71741,"cache_read_input_tokens":15594,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":71741},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6462fec0-efde-4cfe-9af1-9de8cea35db6","uuid":"c71e4f81-4447-409d-b996-123407041331"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_019sfCkNt2r7e7PZyCsZPEsR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Analysis\n\n**Problem**: go-unix-utils is a specification-driven development experiment using classic Unix utilities as the test bed. Formal specs are derived from C source, Go implementations are generated by AI, and correctness is verified via differential testing against GNU reference binaries.\n\n**Architecture**: Four-stage pipeline (extract → specify → synthesize → verify) with `cmd/` per-utility packages, shared `pkg/testutils/` (differential testing harness), `pkg/sys/` (syscall abstractions), `pkg/format/` (output formatting), and `magefiles/` (build targets).\n\n**Current state**: All PRDs (prd001-prd010), use cases, and test suites are written. No Go code has been implemented yet. All work is in the specification phase.\n\n**Current release**: rel01.0 (in_progress) — single use case rel01.0-uc001-ts (timestamp stdin lines). Requires pkg/testutils (shared harness) and cmd/ts.\n\n## Priority Analysis\n\nrel01.0 is the earliest incomplete release. Its dependency chain is: pkg/testutils → cmd/ts. Since pkg/testutils is the foundation for all differential testing and must exist before any utility can be verified, it is the highest priority task. ts requires only pkg/testutils (no pkg/sys or pkg/format per the dependency map).\n\nWith 1 task allowed, the right choice is implementing pkg/testutils — the foundational harness that unblocks everything else."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":71741,"cache_read_input_tokens":15594,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":71741},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6462fec0-efde-4cfe-9af1-9de8cea35db6","uuid":"461a6cc9-f963-40b7-b938-47d5d